MLT 7.42.0 for RISC OS: engine test 5
=====================================

This is the engine of Reel Edit, a video editor for RISC OS that is being
written: the MLT multimedia framework (https://www.mltframework.org), with
its command-line player and renderer, melt. It is here so that MLT can be
tried on real machines before the editor's windows are built on it.

It needs RISC OS 5 on ARMv7 with VFP (Raspberry Pi 2, 3, 4), with
SharedUnixLibrary and ARMEABISupport loaded (!Run checks). H.264 is
decoded and encoded on the Pi's VideoCore, and HEVC decoded on the Pi 4's
HEVC block, through riscos-reelhwaccel, with the ARM taking over for
anything they refuse. 1080p on the VideoCore needs gpu_mem=128 in
config.txt.

Trying it
---------
Most useful with one of your own 1080p videos, at least 20 seconds long:
  *Set MLTtest$Clip SDFS::Pi.$.Videos.clip/mp4
Then double-click !MLT: it starts Test (shift-double-click opens !MLT
instead). Test runs in a TaskWindow, so the desktop keeps working
(please try using it while the test runs). It makes two 1080p
clips and a busy one (random noise) with the VideoCore's encoder, times
decoding the busy clip with FFmpeg alone and through MLT, puts a crossfade
between the other two, times
how fast the editor's preview could make the pictures (VideoCore and ARM
decoding), and exports the result; then the same with your clip.
Everything goes in <Wimp$ScrapDir>.MLTtest. Save the TaskWindow's text
(Menu > Save) and send it back.

mltbench
--------
  mltbench FILE [FRAMES] [SCALE]
times how long each picture of a clip or project takes to make, exactly as
Reel Edit's preview will ask for them (on average, usually (the median),
and the slowest); SCALE 0.5 makes them at half size.
  mltbench -raw FILE [FRAMES]
decodes the file with FFmpeg alone, with the decoder MLT would choose: the
least MLT could cost, to compare with the figure above.

melt
----
Once !MLT has been seen by the Filer, *melt works in any TaskWindow:

  melt clip/mp4 -consumer null
      decode the clip as fast as possible (a speed test)
  melt a/mp4 b/mp4 -mix 25 -mixer luma -consumer avformat:/RAM::RamDisc0/$/out/mp4 vcodec=h264_encvchiq
      two clips with a one second crossfade, saved as H.264
  melt a/mp4 b/mp4 -consumer xml:project/mlt
      the same as an MLT project (XML), which Shotcut and Kdenlive open too

File names are Unix-style when they have a path (UnixLib translates
them): /SDFS::Pi.$.Videos/clip.mp4 is SDFS::Pi.$.Videos.clip/mp4.

What is different from MLT elsewhere
------------------------------------
- No threads: MLT does all its work in the program that asks for it.
  That's what lets a desktop program use it without stopping other tasks.
- The Pi's hardware decoders are tried first for H.264 and HEVC; any clip
  they refuse, at the start or part way through, goes to the ARM decoder.
  Only one clip at a time has the VideoCore's decoder (a second one never
  works with gpu_mem=128): when two clips play together, as in a
  crossfade, the second is decoded on the ARM, and the decoder passes to
  it once the first has finished.
  *Set MLT_AVFORMAT_PREFER none to decode everything on the ARM.
- Modules: core, avformat (FFmpeg) and xml. No Qt, GTK, SDL, frei0r or
  OpenGL effects.

